  "result": "Think of a boss who's locked in a room with no phone, and an assistant outside who can do things.\n\nThe boss slides a note under the door: \"Check the laser cutter booking for Friday.\" The assistant reads it, goes and looks at the calendar, and slides back a note: \"Taken 2–4pm, free otherwise.\" The boss reads that and writes the final answer to the student: \"It's free on Friday except 2–4pm.\"\n\nThe mapping:\n- **Boss** = the model. It can only read and write notes. It never touches the calendar.\n- **Note under the door** = the tool call. It names the tool and the details, like \"check_calendar, Friday, laser cutter\".\n- **Assistant** = your program. It does the actual work.\n- **Note coming back** = the tool result, which the model reads before deciding what to do next.\n\nThe analogy breaks down in one place. The boss is a fresh reader every time and only knows what's on the notes in front of them. That's why this ties straight into context, which is our next topic.\n\nI'm keeping this general because quiz 0001 isn't graded yet. We can go through its items together once it is.\n\nIn your own words, who actually looks at the calendar here?",
